  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Dartmouth College’s Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology 
teaching bibliography for Lesson 24 lists core readings on Late Bronze Age 
Aegean glyptic. Among them are I. Pini’s article “The ‘Ring of Nestor’,” 
published in Oxford Journal of Archaeology 17 (1998) on pages 1–13, and 
immediately following it an entry for I. Pini titled “Further Research on Late 
Bronze Age Aegean Glass Seals,” described as appearing in the volume H 
periphereia tou mykenaïkou kosmou, which is identified as the proceedings of the
A’ International Interdisciplinary Symposium held in Lamia. The positioning of 
these two works together in a short list of key readings indicates that the 
“Further Research” chapter is treated as closely related to, and a continuation 
of, Pini’s study of the same Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic corpus that includes
the Ring of Nestor.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel 
(CMS) project at Heidelberg University maintains a seals bibliography that 
includes entries for studies on Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic. In this 
bibliography, Pini’s “The ‘Ring of Nestor’” is listed with the citation “OJA 
17.1, 1–13,” identifying it as an article in volume 17, issue 1 of the Oxford 
Journal of Archaeology, spanning pages 1–13. The same list also contains a 
separate entry beginning “Further Research on Late Bronze Age Aegean Glass 
Seals. In: E …,” indicating that this is a chapter titled “Further Research on 
Late Bronze Age Aegean Glass Seals” published within an edited volume whose 
editor’s first initial is E. The juxtaposition of the two entries in a 
specialized seals bibliography underscores that both works concern Late Bronze 
Age Aegean seals and that the “Further Research” chapter is a technical 
follow-up to the OJA article within the same corpus.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "E. Tsangaraki’s paper on seal iconography to the 
north of the Mycenaean world, dealing with LH III seals from Mt Olympus in 
Pieria, includes scholarly references to key works on Late Bronze Age Aegean 
glyptic and authenticity debates. In the bibliography or notes, Tsangaraki cites
Pini’s chapter “Further research on Late Bronze Age Aegean glass seals,” giving 
it as appearing in a volume beginning with the Greek title “H …,” and in close 
proximity also cites Vlachopoulos’s chapter “The Ring of Nestor and the Quest 
for Authenticity,” noted with page reference 223 and the name Olga. The pairing 
of these citations shows that Pini’s “Further Research on Late Bronze Age Aegean
Glass Seals” is treated as part of the same thematic body of scholarship as 
Vlachopoulos’s study of the Ring of Nestor, namely research on Late Bronze Age 
Aegean seals and questions of their authenticity and iconography.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "An academic thesis on seal iconography hosted in the 
Pergamos digital library of the National and Kapodistrian University of Athens 
discusses motifs such as lions, griffins, and sphinxes on seals and engages with
major studies on Aegean glyptic. Its bibliography includes a reference to a 
Journal of Hellenic Studies article about the “Ring of Nestor,” cited with a 
title beginning “Ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
sepulchral …,” and also includes an entry for Pini’s “Further Research on Late 
Bronze Age Aegean Glass Seals,” given in Greek as “‘Further Research on Late 
Bronze Age Aegean Glass Seals’, στο: Η. Περιφέρεια …” to indicate its 
publication within a volume titled H periphereia. The simultaneous citation of 
the JHS “Ring of Nestor” article and Pini’s “Further Research” chapter in a 
study of seal iconography indicates that Pini’s follow-up work is considered 
directly relevant to the same iconographic and technical questions surrounding 
Late Bronze Age Aegean glass seals, a category in which the Ring of Nestor is 
treated as a paradigmatic example.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    }
  ],
