   it as "A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World" and refers to "a Sepulchral    
   Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,"          
   indicating a focus on afterlife imagery and a funerary treasure from Thisbê. 
   [14]                                                                         
 • The helladic.info PKey Report states that a "gold treasure discovered in     
   1915" at Thisbê is "described in ... 'The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the 
   Minoan After-World ... Journal of Hellenic Studies (45:1) 1-75. 1925 ...".   
   [15]                                                                         
 • The SciSpace metadata lists the work as: "The ring of Nestor : a glimpse into
   the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and    
   bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," author "Arthur Evans," publication "The    
   Journal of Hellenic Studies," year 1925. [16]                                
 • In the reference list reproduced in the OUCI record, Evans’s work is cited as
   "The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan ... Journal of Hellenic       
   Studies, 45(1), 1–75. 1925". [17]                                            
 • The Syndesmoi 4 bibliography entry reads in part: "EVANS, A., 'The Ring of   
