 • Arthur J. Evans authored an article titled "'The Ring of Nestor': A Glimpse  
   into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings   
   and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," published in The Journal of Hellenic   
   Studies, volume 45, issue 1, in 1925, spanning pages 1–75.                   
   [14][15][16][17][18]                                                         
 • Evans’s 1925 Journal of Hellenic Studies article on "The Ring of Nestor"     
   examines a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from the  
   1915 discovery at Thisbê in Boeotia, interpreting their imagery as a glimpse 
   into the Minoan after-world and discussing their findspot, funerary context, 
   iconography, and dating. [14][15][18]                                        

Ring of Nestor archaeological object discovery context excavation               

 • The article reports that in 2015 a University of Cincinnati archaeological   
   team discovered an undisturbed Bronze Age warrior’s grave near Pylos in      
   southwestern Greece, dating to around 1500 B.C., containing numerous gold    
   objects including four large gold signet rings. [19]                         
 • The UC Magazine feature describes the iconography of the rings, including a  
   bull-leaping scene and a scene involving a goddess and a shrine, and         
   emphasizes that the imagery is Minoan in style though found in a Mycenaean   
   burial. [19]                                                                 
 • The article situates the Griffin Warrior tomb close to the archaeological    
   remains traditionally associated with the Palace of Nestor and states that   
   the find provides new evidence about early Mycenaean ruling elites and their 
   relationship with Crete. [19]                                                
 • Davis and Stocker’s Hesperia article states that in May 2015 a joint team    
   from the University of Cincinnati and the Greek Archaeological Service       
   discovered an undisturbed slab-covered tomb during explorations near the     
   Palace of Nestor at Pylos. [20]                                              
 • The paper identifies the grave as containing a male skeleton and rich grave  
   goods including four large gold signet rings, and interprets the number and  
   quality of the rings as indicative of high elite status. [20]                
 • The article provides measurements and technical descriptions of each ring    
   (Ring 1–4), discusses their engraving techniques, and analyzes iconographic  
   themes such as a goddess and shrine and bull-leaping, concluding that the    
   rings are of Minoan manufacture reused in a Mycenaean context. [20]          
 • Through stylistic comparison and literary discussion, the Hesperia article   
   dates the Griffin Warrior tomb to early LH I and relates it to processes of  
   power formation preceding the fully developed palace era at Pylos. [20]      
 • The Academia.edu PDF version states that the grave lies only tens of meters  
   from the so-called Palace of Nestor and that it is among the best-preserved  
   early Mycenaean elite tombs discovered at Pylos. [21]                        
 • In the detailed catalogue, the authors number the four gold signet rings as  
   Ring 1–4 and provide quantitative data such as weight and dimensions, then   
   compare these rings to Cretan and mainland examples to infer production      
   centers and circulation routes. [21]                                         
 • The discussion section suggests that the rings’ imagery may reflect the roles
   of the deceased and his group in religious rituals and could express concepts
   of female divinity and royal legitimacy. [21]                                
 • The conclusion of the Academia.edu version argues that the Griffin Warrior   
   tomb and its gold rings transform scholarly understanding of interaction     
   between Late Minoan Crete and early Mycenaean elites, demonstrating mainland 
   appropriation and recontextualization of a Minoan iconographic repertoire.   
   [21]                                                                         
 • The Archaeology & Arts news story reports that an undisturbed warrior’s tomb 
   was discovered near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos Chora, containing weapons, 
   jewelry, and gold artifacts, and dates the burial to approximately the       
   17th–16th centuries B.C. [22]                                                
 • The article highlights four solid-gold rings among the grave goods and notes 
   that one is engraved with a Cretan-style bull-leaping scene. [22]            
 • The news piece states that the number of gold signet rings in this single    
   Mycenaean grave exceeds that found in any other known Mycenaean tomb and     
   cites the Greek Ministry of Culture in emphasizing the find’s importance for 
   understanding the pre-palatial history of the Palace of Nestor site. [22]    
 • Archaeology magazine’s feature recounts that during a 2015 surface survey    
   Davis and Stocker’s team noticed an unusual stone slab in an olive grove and,
   upon excavation, uncovered a shaft grave beneath containing a male skeleton  
   and more than 1,500 grave goods. [23]                                        
 • The article describes a gold ring showing several women standing before a    
   columned shrine, with trees and birds in the background, and interprets the  
   scene as a ritual at a palace sanctuary or temple related to palace religion.
   [23]                                                                         
 • The feature notes that this ring is often referred to as the “Ring of Nestor”
   because it is thought to be symbolically linked to the architecture or       
   frescoes of the Palace of Nestor and to express a close connection between   
   the tomb’s owner and the palace center. [23]                                 
 • Drawing on Davis and Stocker, the article argues that the Minoan-style gold  
   rings and other luxury objects in the grave indicate that the Griffin Warrior
   played a major political role in a society not yet fully organized around    
   palace institutions and that possession of such items served to assert his   
   power and cultural capital. [23]                                             
 • The ResearchGate abstract states that the Griffin Warrior tomb was discovered
   near the Palace of Nestor in southwestern Greece and that its grave goods    
   include weapons, jewelry, and four gold signet rings. [24]                   
 • The abstract notes that the article discusses the excavation, artistic style,
   and craftsmanship of the rings and that the iconography of the gold signet   
   rings provides evidence for the ideology and religious beliefs of the        
   deceased and his community. [24]                                             
 • The ResearchGate entry gives the full citation for the article as Hesperia   
   85.4 (2016), 627–655, and lists keywords including Pylos, Griffin Warrior,   
   gold rings, and Late Bronze Age. [24]                                        
 • The archaeological object known as the “Ring of Nestor” is one of four large 
   gold signet rings recovered in 2015 from the undisturbed Griffin Warrior     
   shaft grave near the so‑called Palace of Nestor at Pylos; the grave contained
   a male skeleton and over 1,500 grave goods including weapons, jewelry, and   
   numerous gold objects, and is dated by scholarly analysis to early LH I      
   (around the mid‑2nd millennium B.C.). [19][20][21][22][23][24]               
 • Within the Griffin Warrior grave, four large solid‑gold signet rings         
   (including the so‑called Ring of Nestor) were found concentrated near the    
   body of the deceased male, and this unusually high number and quality of     
   rings in a single Mycenaean tomb is interpreted as marking him as a          
   high‑status elite. [19][20][22][24]                                          
 • The four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb, including the Ring 
   of Nestor, are catalogued in detail (as Ring 1–4) with measurements and      
   technical descriptions; their iconography features Minoan‑style themes such  
   as bull‑leaping scenes and a goddess before a columned shrine with trees and 
   birds, and they are generally regarded as Minoan creations reused in a       
   Mycenaean funerary context. [19][20][21][22][23]                             
 • The gold ring referred to as the “Ring of Nestor” bears a scene showing      
   several women standing before a columned shrine with trees and birds, and is 
   so named because scholars see symbolic links between its imagery and the     
   architecture or frescoes of the Palace of Nestor, using this to argue for a  
   close connection between the tomb’s owner and the palace center. [23]        
 • Scholarly discussions of the Ring of Nestor and the other gold signet rings  
   from the Griffin Warrior tomb argue that their Minoan‑style imagery and      
   workmanship, found in a Mycenaean burial near the Palace of Nestor, provide  
   important evidence for cultural interaction between Late Minoan Crete and    
   early Mycenaean elites and for processes of power formation in a pre‑palatial
   society at Pylos. [19][20][21][23][24]                                       

Ring of Nestor gold ring physical description iconography scholarly analysis    

 • 文章对“Ring of                                                               
   Nestor”印面场景进行分区描述，指出其中包括船只、树木、祭坛、以及神祇或女性人物
   等要素，并将这些要素与弥诺斯宗教与仪式场景的图像传统进行比较。 [1]           
 • 论文列举了将“Ring of                                                         
   Nestor”视为伪作的理由，如认为戒指的图像题材组合过于完备或呈现“选集式”搭配，并
   将这些理由纳入爱琴世界伪作讨论的更大框架中。 [1]                             
 • Vlachopoulos 指出，“Ring of Nestor”为金质印章戒指，印面约 3.29×2.18          
   厘米，并将其列为已知爱琴世界中第五大金质图章戒指，强调其异常大的体量。 [8]   
 • 该章节通过对金属工艺、浮雕技法和图像传统的比较分析，得出结论认为“Ring of     
   Nestor”的现有证据更支持其真实性，并反驳早期提出的近代伪作假说。 [8]          
 • Vlachopoulos                                                                 
   将戒指印面划分为包含船或舟、树或圣树、祭坛与供品、岩石或贝特尔石以及女性人物 
   或女神等图像区域，用以讨论弥诺斯宗教空间和神祇显现场景。 [8]                 
 • 该章节包含对“Ring of                                                         
   Nestor”收藏史与现存位置的梳理，叙述其从发现到纳入博物馆收藏体系的过程，并讨论
   涉及的法律和出处问题。 [8]                                                   
 • 《Traces of Places》一文将“Ring of                                           
   Nestor”用作弥诺斯金戒指上缩微圣地场景的例证，指出其图像中可能包含座位、祭坛和
   树木，并与其他戒指上类似组合进行对比。 [25]                                  
 • 论文提出弥诺斯金戒指构成“便携圣地”，佩戴者通过戒指上组合神祇、建筑、自然元素 
   和祭祀设施的图像，象征性携带或再现特定圣地，“Ring of Nestor”被纳入这一解释。 
   [25]                                                                         
 • “Fakes and Dubitanda”章节描述“Ring of                                        
   Nestor”印面图像为在船上的女性人物、祭坛、圣树与贝特尔石等主题的组合，并指出这
   种高密度与“选集式”拼贴被视为质疑其真实性的证据。 [26]                        
 • 该章节在回顾工艺和风格证据后，主张尽管“Ring of                               
   Nestor”存在诸多疑点，但在当前证据下不应轻率地将其归为伪作。 [26]             
 • 讨论 Isopata 戒指的论文在分析神祇显现叙事模式时，多次以“Ring of              
   Nestor”为平行例证，强调其图像中女性人物、船只、祭坛和树木的组合可被理解为宗教
   仪式与神祇临在的场景。 [6]                                                   
 • 该论文引用埃文斯等早期出版物，包括对“Ring of                                 
   Nestor”印面的绘图和说明，指出这些早期图像资料构成后续对戒指图像学解读的重要基
   础。 [6]                                                                     
 • The Ring of Nestor is described in scholarship as a gold signet ring with a  
   seal face measuring about 3.29 x 2.18 cm, and as unusually large among known 
   Aegean gold signet rings. [8]                                                
 • Scholarly descriptions of the Ring of Nestor's seal image identify a         
   multi-element scene that includes a ship or boat, trees or a sacred tree, an 
   altar with offerings, and a female figure or deity; some analyses also       
   include a rock or betyl element. [1][8][25][26][6]                           
 • The Ring of Nestor is interpreted in scholarship as a miniature sacred-site  
   or portable-sanctuary image that stages religious ritual and divine presence,
   while its dense and seemingly curated composition has also been cited by some
   authors as evidence for doubts about authenticity. [1][8][25][26]            

