 • The artifact known as the “Ring of Nestor” is a Late Minoan I gold signet    
   ring in the Ashmolean Museum, catalogued under the museum number AN1938.1130 
   and bearing that popular name in the Ashmolean Image Library. [27][28]       
 • Catalog data for the Ashmolean Museum’s signet ring AN1938.1130 (the “Ring of
   Nestor”) record its material as gold, with approximate dimensions hoop       
   diameter 2.5 cm, bezel 3.5 × 2.2 cm, and weight 31.8 g. [27]                 
 • Heritage Images, using Ashmolean Museum information, describes the “Ring of  
   Nestor” (AN1938.1130) as a Late Minoan I signet ring depicting four cult or  
   ritual scenes. [27]                                                          

Ring of Nestor dating cultural attribution significance Aegean archaeology      

 • Under Aegean glyptic the Dartmouth Lesson 24 bibliography lists: “Pini, ‘The 
   ‘Ring of Nestor’’, OJA 17 (1998) 1–13.” [4]                                  
 • The same bibliography includes a section on “Aegean and Cypro‑Aegean         
   Non‑Sphragistic Decorated Gold Finger Rings” and lists works on Minoan and   
   Mycenaean gold rings, placing the Ring of Nestor within this thematic        
   grouping. [4]                                                                
 • The abstract of Pini’s article notes that “In the past the authenticity of   
   the so‑called Ring of Nestor has been debated mostly in terms of iconography.
   Arguments against its ancient origin rest …” and that the study reassesses   
   these arguments. [3]                                                         
 • Secondary descriptions of Pini’s article indicate that it “belongs to a group
   of Late Bronze Age Aegean gold signet rings,” and that Pini compares the Ring
   of Nestor’s iconography and workmanship with securely provenanced Minoan and 
   Mycenaean pieces to evaluate its authenticity. [3]                           
 • The preprint’s abstract states that it “presents an analysis of the          
   iconography surrounding the ‘Ring of Nestor’ in the context of Aegean        
   forgeries and authenticity debates.” [1]                                     
 • According to the summary, the preprint situates the ring within “Late Bronze 
   Age Aegean glyptic motifs,” comparing its scene and figure types to other    
   rings with processional or cult imagery from Minoan and Mycenaean contexts.  
   [1]                                                                          
 • The preprint discusses “the ‘Minoan dragon,’ tree and underworld or afterlife
   elements seen on the ring,” relating these motifs to religious symbolism and 
   to questions of cultural attribution and chronological placement. [1]        
 • Palmer writes that “The ‘Ring of Minos’ (AM 1938.1110) and ‘Ring of Nestor’  
   (VI.277) have both been denounced as fakes by Nilsson (1950: 42–49) and      
   Biesantz (1954⁷: 115–119) …”. [29]                                           
 • In Palmer’s catalogue the Ring of Nestor appears as “VI.277” in the Corpus   
   der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS). [29]                            
 • Palmer situates the Ring of Nestor within a broader pattern of “composite    
   creatures on seals and sealings,” using its hybrid imagery and composition to
   discuss religious symbolism and stylistic phases of the Late Bronze Age      
   Aegean. [29]                                                                 
 • The chapter states: “On the Ring of Nestor, a Minoan Dragon can also be seen 
   in a natural setting. It is standing on a grassy ground in front of the trunk
   of the tree whose branches …”. [30]                                          
 • Within its discussion of hybrid creatures, the chapter identifies the hybrid 
   animal on the Ring of Nestor as a “Minoan Dragon” and considers it alongside 
   examples from Knossos and mainland contexts. [30]                            
 • In the bibliography of the metal study appears the entry: “‘The Ring of      
   Nestor’: A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world and a sepulchral treasure of  
   gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia. JHS 45, 1–75.” [31]   
 • The cited title characterizes the material as “a sepulchral treasure of gold 
   signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” and frames the Ring of     
   Nestor as offering “a glimpse into the Minoan after‑world.” [31]             
 • Scholarly analyses generally situate the Ring of Nestor within the repertoire
   of Late Bronze Age Aegean gold signet rings and glyptic, comparing its       
   iconography and workmanship with Minoan and Mycenaean rings. [4][3][1]       
 • The Ring of Nestor’s imagery, including a hybrid creature identified as a    
   ‘Minoan dragon’, a tree, and possible underworld or afterlife elements, is   
   interpreted by several scholars within a Minoan religious or afterlife       
   symbolic context. [1][29][30][31]                                            
 • The Ring of Nestor has been the subject of authenticity debates, with        
   mid‑20th‑century scholars such as Nilsson and Biesantz denouncing it as a    
   fake, and later studies (e.g., Pini 1998 and subsequent iconographic         
   preprints) reassessing these arguments and treating it within the corpus of  
   Late Bronze Age Aegean gold signet rings. [3][1][29]                         
 • Modern scholarship often treats the Ring of Nestor under the standard Aegean 
   glyptic corpus Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS) as entry   
   VI.277, indicating its working classification among Minoan–Mycenaean seals   
   and rings despite ongoing authenticity and cultural attribution debates. [29]
 • The Ring of Nestor has been connected, at least in one major study, with a   
   sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê in       
   Boeotia, implying a claimed funerary association and mainland find context   
   for the material discussed. [31]                                             

